{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "Track TCS Guidance Accuracy and Build Management Credibility Score",
  "scenario": "You're a senior tech analyst covering TCS on the Mumbai desk. Over 10 quarters (2.5 years), TCS has issued quarterly guidance on revenue growth, margin targets, and hiring commitments. Your job: build a guidance-tracking scorecard that compares what management *said would happen* against what *actually happened*, then score their credibility for use in your next forecast.",
  "instructions": "For each quarter, calculate the guidance miss/beat as a percentage, assess the bias (conservative or optimistic), count how many times management quietly walked back a prior commitment, and produce a final credibility score (0–100) that reflects whether TCS management can be trusted on future guidance.",
  "learning_objective": "Calculate guidance accuracy by comparing actual results to prior guidance across multiple quarters, identify the pattern of over-promise vs under-promise, and assign a credibility score that weights future guidance by the team's track record of delivery.",
  "hints": [
    "Start simple: calculate the percentage difference between Actual and Guided for each quarter using (Actual − Guided) ÷ Guided.",
    "A positive difference means they beat; negative means they missed. Count how many quarters beat vs missed to spot the bias.",
    "A team that consistently misses by 2–3% each quarter has low credibility; a team that beats or is within 1% has high credibility. Weight the credibility score accordingly."
  ],
